  "typecheckAfterFollowUp": true,
  "summary": "The clock app is built. It typechecks, builds, and a headless render showed the whole clock in frame with no errors. I did not check the motion frame by frame, so the 2-second period, the one-tick-per-second second hand and the real-time hands are verified only from the code.\n\n- **Static parts:** `recipes/clockcase.mjs` makes the wall, floor, skirting, wooden case, hood, glass-like pane, round dial, bezel and 12 hour marks. I imported it with `import_three`, which produced `src/three-bridge/clockcase.*`.\n- **Moving parts:** `src/main.ts` adds the hour, minute and second hands, the pendulum and the two hanging weights as native Aura3D nodes, with warm interior point lights over the `interior-warm` look. No external model files are loaded.\n- **Pendulum:** it swings as `0.16·sin(2πt/2)`, so the period is 2 seconds and it is slowest at the extremes.\n- **Hands:** the second hand steps once per second. The minute and hour hands follow the local time from `Date`.\n- **Weights:** both sink 0.004 units per second and stop at a floor position.\n- **Camera and start-up:** the camera is front-facing at y 2.4, slightly above the clock's centre. `window.__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first presented frame. I also added `window.__TASK_STATE__`, which you didn't ask for; it holds the current hand angles, pendulum angle and weight height.\n\nThe render tool warns that the glass pane casts a shadow as if it were opaque. The pane looks grey over the dark back panel rather than clear.\n\n"
}